      "text_raw": "The question arises which\namplifier would be better,\nthe first one or the second,\nwhere the same current is\nused but now with transistor\nM2 in parallel with M1?\nClearly in the second\namplifier, the output resis-\ntance is higher and so is the\ngain. Accordingly, the band-\nwidth will be smaller.\nLet us have a closer look\nat this amplifier. Normally\nthe current source is realized\nby means of another transis-\ntor, a pMOST device, the\ngate of which is connected to a voltage reference, which sets all DC currents.\nWe still have two possibilities as shown next.",
      "text": "The question arises which amplifier would be better, the first one or the second, where the same current is used but now with transistor M2 in parallel with M1? Clearly in the second amplifier, the output resistance is higher and so is the gain. Accordingly, the bandwidth will be smaller. Let us have a closer look at this amplifier. Normally the current source is realized by means of another transistor, a pMOST device, the gate of which is connected to a voltage reference, which sets all DC currents. We still have two possibilities as shown next."
